.TH smi-aws-eks-create-tf "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-aws-eks-create-tf \- Print an OpenTofu and Terraform configuration that creates a Kubernetes cluster on AWS from an empty account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-aws-eks-create-tf
.I CLIENT_NAME
.RI [ AWS_REGION ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Prints, on standard output, one configuration file that builds a running Amazon EKS Kubernetes
cluster in an AWS account where nothing exists yet. Nothing is created by this command: the
output is saved as
.I main.tf
and applied with
.BR tofu (1)
or
.BR terraform (1),
which read the same language; it uses AWS provider resources only, no external modules, so all
that is created is in the one file.
.PP
It creates:
.TP
.B Network
A VPC of its own, an internet gateway, a public and a private subnet in each of two availability
zones, one NAT gateway, and the routes between them. The subnets carry the tags Kubernetes looks
for when it places internet facing and internal load balancers.
.TP
.B Firewall
A security group opening HTTP and HTTPS for web load balancers.
.TP
.B Cluster
The IAM roles of the control plane and of the nodes, the EKS cluster with access entries as its
authentication, and the control plane audit and authenticator logs in CloudWatch Logs. Whoever
runs the apply becomes administrator of the cluster.
.TP
.B Nodes
A managed node group in the private subnets, two nodes by default, scaling between one and three,
on Amazon Linux 2023.
.TP
.B Add-ons
vpc-cni, kube-proxy, coredns and eks-pod-identity-agent, managed and updated by EKS.
.PP
The client name and region are written in as variable defaults. Everything else is a variable
with a default as well: address range, number of availability zones, Kubernetes version,
addresses allowed to the API, instance types, spot or on demand capacity, image, disk size and
node counts, changed with
.B -var
or a
.I terraform.tfvars
file.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I CLIENT_NAME
Lower case letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter and not ending with a hyphen, at
most 32 characters, so every name built from it fits the AWS limits. It is the name of the
cluster and the prefix of every other name.
.TP
.I AWS_REGION
Default
.BR eu-central-1 .
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.PP
OpenTofu or Terraform 1.6 or newer,
.BR aws (1)
with credentials allowed to create VPC, EC2, IAM and EKS resources, and
.BR kubectl (1)
to use the cluster. The state is kept in the working directory, so that directory has to be kept,
or a backend configured before the first apply when several people manage the cluster.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
The Kubernetes API is reachable from every address by default, protected by AWS authentication;
.B cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs
restricts it to the given addresses. The nodes have no public address and reach the internet only
through the NAT gateway.
.SH COSTS
.PP
An EKS cluster, a NAT gateway with its address and the nodes are charged by the hour even when
idle.
.B tofu destroy
or
.B terraform destroy
in the same directory removes everything again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The configuration was printed.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong number of arguments, an invalid client name or an invalid region.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Create a cluster and use it:
.RS
.nf
mkdir acme && cd acme
smi-aws-eks-create-tf acme eu-north-1 > main.tf
tofu init
tofu apply
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region eu-north-1 --name acme
kubectl get nodes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Three availability zones, larger spot nodes and the API open to one office only:
.RS
.nf
tofu apply -var availability_zone_count=3 \e
    -var 'node_instance_types=["m6i.large"]' -var node_capacity_type=SPOT \e
    -var 'cluster_endpoint_public_access_cidrs=["203.0.113.10/32"]'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aws (1),
.BR kubectl (1),
.BR smi-postgresql-create-db-sql (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
